/**
 * @page low_speed_io Low speed interfaces
 * 
 * @if DOX_NAV
 * @ref dev_config "Back to the list of SDR device configuration sub-topics"  
 * @endif
 * 
 * @section low_speed_io_summary Summary
 * 
 * Low speed interfaces can be used to bypass library functions and communicate directly with SDR device modules. Currently, library device interface class supports two low speed interfaces:
 * SPI and I2C. The SPI interface is the primary interface used for communications with modules on SDR device. Current I2C interface implementation exists for compatibility reasons and is not widely
 * adopted by SDR boards. To test if interface for a particular SDR device is available, call the respective interface function. If the interface is not implemented, a warning message will be 
 * logged and @ref lime::OpStatus::NotImplemented "NotImplemented" operation status number will be returned.
 * 
 * @if RST_SUPPORT
 * @embedRstVerbatim{
 * .. admonition:: Note
 * 
 *    Alternatively, LMS7002M and FPGA module registers can also be accessed using dedicated register write/read functions described in SDR device configuration sub-topic - @ref direct_config.}
 * @else
 * @note Alternatively, LMS7002M and FPGA module registers can also be accessed using dedicated register write/read functions described in SDR device configuration sub-topic - @ref direct_config.
 * @endif
 * 
 * @subsection low_speed_io_spi Serial Peripheral Interface (SPI)
 * 
 * SPI interface is widely adopted by the SDR devices that are supported by the library. SPI interface allows to directly access all of the control and status registers of SDR device modules. 
 * Information about each module registers is provided in the respective data sheets of the modules. 
 * 
 * Different SDR device models, can use a different amount and different types of modules with unique IDs. Unique module IDs are used to identify each module and must be passed as an argument to 
 * @ref lime::SDRDevice::SPI(uint32_t, const uint32_t*, uint32_t*, uint32_t) "SPI()" function ``spiBusAddress`` parameter that acts as a chip select to target the correct module. To find out 
 * about all of the SDR device modules and their IDs, it is recommended to retrieve SDR device @ref lime::SDRDevice::GetDescriptor() "descriptor" and extract the list of available modules and 
 * the list of their SPI slave IDs. Below is a simple example code snippet, that extracts the list of available modules and their respective SPI slave IDs:
 * 
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <iostream>
 * #include <iomanip>
 * #include <vector>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceRegistry.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/SDRDevice.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceHandle.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/OpStatus.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/SDRDescriptor.h>
 * 
 * int main()
 * {
 *    std::vector<lime::DeviceHandle> listOfDevices = lime::DeviceRegistry::enumerate();
 *    if(listOfDevices.empty())
 *    {
 *       std::cout << "No devices detected!\n";
 *       return 0;
 *    }
 * 
 *    lime::SDRDevice * device = lime::DeviceRegistry::makeDevice(listOfDevices.front());
 *    if(device == nullptr)
 *    {
 *       std::cout << "Failed to connect to SDR device\n";
 *       return 1;
 *    }
 * 
 *    uint8_t moduleIndex = 0;
 *    lime::SDRDescriptor deviceDesc = device->GetDescriptor();
 *    std::cout << "SPI slave IDs of " << deviceDesc.name << " device:" << std::endl;
 *    for(const auto& iter : deviceDesc.spiSlaveIds)
 *    {
 *       std::cout << "\tModule: " << std::setw(10) << iter.first 
 *       << std::setw(10) << "ID: " << iter.second << std::endl;
 *    }
 *  
 *    if(deviceDesc.spiSlaveIds.size() == 0)
 *       std::cout << "No SPI slave IDs present" << std::endl;
 *    
 *    lime::DeviceRegistry::freeDevice(device);
 *    return 0;
 * }
 * @endcode
 * 
 * Output of the example code for a LimeSDR USB device:
 * 
 * @image{inline} html dev_spi_slave_ids.png
 * 
 */